cugpt — C Translation of Karpathy's microgpt
=============================================

Two C implementations of Andrej Karpathy's microgpt.py: a minimal,
dependency-free GPT that trains a character-level language model on a
names dataset and generates hallucinated names at inference time.

  microgpt.py  — original pure-Python reference by Karpathy
  microgpt.c   — C translation using a Wengert tape for autograd;
                 zero dependencies beyond libc/libm
  cugpt.c      — C translation with manual backward pass and
                 OpenBLAS-accelerated matrix-vector products

MODEL ARCHITECTURE
------------------

  Architecture  1-layer GPT-2 variant
  Params        4,192
  vocab_size    27  (26 lowercase letters + BOS token)
  n_embd        16
  n_head        4   (head_dim = 4)
  block_size    16
  Activation    ReLU (not GeLU)
  Norm          RMSNorm (no learnable scale/bias)
  Optimizer     Adam  lr=0.01 β1=0.85 β2=0.99, linear LR decay
  Steps         1,000 on Karpathy's names dataset

QUICK START
-----------

  make
  ./microgpt
  ./cugpt
  python3 microgpt.py     # reference (slow)

All three download input.txt automatically on first run.

BENCHMARK RESULTS
-----------------

Machine: Ubuntu 22.04 / WSL2, gcc 14.2.0, Python 3.13.3
Task: 1,000 training steps on names dataset (same algorithm, same data)

Python baseline:

  python3 microgpt.py                               146.11 s

microgpt.c — compiler flag sweep:

  Flags                                             Time (s)   Speedup vs Python
  -O0                                                  1.592       92×
  -O1                                                  0.760      192×
  -O2                                                  0.718      203×
  -O3                                                  0.729      200×
  -O2 -march=native                                    0.588      248×
  -O3 -march=native                                    0.637      229×
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math                        0.337      434×
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -funroll-loops         0.323      452×  ← best
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -flto                  0.336      435×
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -funroll-loops -flto   0.332      440×

cugpt.c — compiler flag sweep:

  Flags                                             Time (s)   Speedup vs Python
  -O0                                                  0.189      773×
  -O1                                                  0.090     1623×
  -O2                                                  0.078     1873×
  -O3                                                  0.073     2001×
  -O2 -march=native                                    0.077     1897×
  -O3 -march=native                                    0.082     1782×
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math                        0.061     2395×
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -funroll-loops         0.078     1873×
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -flto                  0.059     2476×  ← best
  -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -funroll-loops -flto   0.064     2283×

Summary:

  Implementation                     Best time   Speedup vs Python
  ─────────────────────────────────  ─────────   ─────────────────
  microgpt.py (Python)               146.11 s         1×
  microgpt.c  (tape autograd)          0.323 s       452×
  cugpt.c     (BLAS + manual grad)     0.059 s      2476×

Key observations:

  • -ffast-math provides the biggest single-flag win (≈2× for both files).
    It allows the compiler to reassociate floating-point ops and replace
    library calls with faster approximations — safe here because we only
    need single-step loss descent, not bit-exact reproducibility.

  • -funroll-loops helps microgpt.c (small inner loops over tape entries)
    but slightly hurts cugpt.c (BLAS already handles inner loops).

  • -flto (link-time optimization) wins for cugpt.c by inlining the BLAS
    wrapper functions across translation units.

  • -march=native alone gives modest gains (248× vs 203× for microgpt.c);
    the real win comes from combining it with -ffast-math.

  • cugpt.c is 5.5× faster than microgpt.c at their respective bests.
    The advantage: no tape allocation/traversal overhead, and BLAS kernels
    exploit SIMD for the linear algebra that dominates the compute.

  • Even the unoptimized cugpt.c (-O0) beats the best microgpt.c build,
    because eliminating the autograd tape is an algorithmic win, not just
    a compiler win.

The default Makefile build uses the empirically fastest flags:

  microgpt: -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -funroll-loops
  cugpt:    -O3 -march=native -ffast-math -flto

IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
--------------------

microgpt.c
  Translates the Python scalar-Value autograd graph to a flat Wengert
  tape: a pre-allocated arena of 4M TapeEntry structs reset each step.
  Backward is a single reverse scan with no pointer chasing or heap
  allocation. This is the same structural speedup described in comments
  inside the Python original ("~4x speedup even in Python").

cugpt.c
  Discards the autograd tape entirely. Forward activations are saved
  in a struct array (Activations act[BLOCK_SIZE]) and the backward pass
  is written analytically. Every matrix-vector product is dispatched
  through cblas_dgemv; weight gradient accumulation uses cblas_dger
  (rank-1 outer product update). The result is ~5.5× faster than the
  tape version at peak optimization.

BUILDING
--------

  make            # both binaries, best flags
  make check      # smoke test (10 steps each)
  make bench      # full flag sweep + Python baseline
  make install    # to /usr/local (or PREFIX=...)
  make dist       # creates cugpt-1.0.tar.gz

See INSTALL for dependency details and manual build instructions.

